hacktheplanet — CTF / HTB / authorized-engagement command reference
==========================================================================
Command-first field reference: copy-paste syntax, per service / per port. The
phase -> ATT&CK -> tool MAP is OFFENSIVE-METHODOLOGY.md; the working METHOD (the
recon loop, the unsticking playbook) is ~/ippsec (`ipp`); the blue-team mirror
(detections + event IDs) is PURPLE-TEAM.md.

Folds in vim/neovim (modeline at the bottom wires it): `za` toggle a fold, `zM`
close all, `zR` open all.

A few command blocks sit between `# companion:gen ID` / `# companion:end ID`
markers: those are GENERATED from the structured companion (offensive/companion/
entries/red/), which is canonical for them — edit the entry and run
offensive/companion/gen-views.sh, not the lines here (CI rejects a hand-edit).
Everything outside the markers is hand-authored as usual.

Most of the corpus is NOT projected here — 18 of its 103 red entries are (23 of 101
blue in PURPLE-TEAM.md). That's deliberate, for three reasons, plus one honest gap:

  1. The projected slice is the on-prem AD/Windows OVERLAP, and even inside it some
     entries stay unprojected because the prose here is the richer superset — the
     sharper token (`<dc-ip>` not the projected `<ip_address>`), inline pedagogy, and
     follow-up chain steps the entry omits. adcs-esc1-certipy, coerce-petitpotam,
     ntlm-relay-ntlmrelayx, pth-lateral-nxc, smb-enum-nxc and reverse-tunnel-chisel
     are covered that way below. (rdp-hijack-tscon is the weak member: its two
     commands are byte-identical to the ones below — equal, not richer.)
  2. The corpus is a deliberate SUPERSET: 56 red entries are cloud / SaaS / CI-CD
     (AWS, Entra, GCP, Okta, Snowflake, k8s, Jenkins, GitHub/GitLab, npm/PyPI, Vault,
     Terraform Cloud, Cloudflare, Harbor, Google Workspace, Slack) and 13 more are
     C2-egress / Impact. Those have NO counterpart here and never will — this file is
     organized per-service / per-port, and cloud has no port. Read that half with
     `htpx` (~/companion), which pairs each attack beside its detection. Same on the
     blue side: PURPLE-TEAM.md is the Windows Security / Sysmon mirror, so the
     cloud-audit-log detections live only in the corpus too.
  3. 7 red entries are Linux persistence / privesc / credential-access. This file's
     Linux material is organized around getting a shell and stabilizing it, not
     around post-exploitation on a Linux host, so those live in the corpus as well.

  ⚠ That leaves TWO unaccounted: bloodhound-collect and ldap-recon (both
     `section: Active Directory — discovery`, both `platform: [windows, network]`).
     They are squarely inside reason 1's subject area, are not in its list, and are
     projected nowhere — so they are a real gap in the projection, not a category.
     Their blue pairs (bloodhound-collect-4662, ldap-recon-4662) are the same gap on
     the other side: both key off event 4662, which is PURPLE-TEAM.md's own criterion
     for projecting, and neither projects. Read them with `htpx` until that is fixed.
     ⚠ bloodhound-collect additionally invokes `bloodhound-python`, the LEGACY (<=4.3.1)
     collector whose zips do NOT ingest into BloodHound CE — see the BloodHound fold
     below, which uses bloodhound-ce-python. Fix routes upstream to htpx.

So an unprojected red entry is richer prose here (1), corpus-only tradecraft this file
doesn't cover (2, 3) — or one of the two known gaps above.

These counts are hand-maintained and go stale on every `companion-sync`: they last
moved for htpx v2.10.0, which added 11 red and 11 blue. If they disagree with
`ls offensive/companion/entries/red | wc -l`, the file is wrong, not the corpus.

SCOPE: authorized engagements (written ROE + scope) or your own CTF/HTB labs ONLY.
Engagement DATA lives in ~/engagements (`mkengagement`), never in this repo.

Target fill — NEVER in this buffer. `htp` opens this file READ-ONLY on purpose:
~/hacktheplanet is a symlink to a TRACKED file in a public repo, so substituting a
real client IP / hostname / domain here and pressing `:w` publishes engagement data.
.gitignore cannot save you — the file is already tracked.

Fill a COPY inside the engagement instead (needs $ENGAGEMENT — mkengagement/eng):
    sed -e 's,<ip_address>,10.10.10.10,g' \
        -e 's,<hostname>,box.htb,g'       \
        -e 's,<domain>,corp.local,g'      \
        ~/hacktheplanet > "$ENGAGEMENT/hacktheplanet.filled"
    ${EDITOR:-nvim} "$ENGAGEMENT/hacktheplanet.filled"

  TARGET ids worth filling:  <ip_address> <hostname> <domain> <user> <password>
  inline one-offs:  <your-ip> (YOUR listener IP, never the target) <port> <NThash> <share> <rid>/<sid>

To edit the reference ITSELF — fixing a command, adding a section — open it for
writing deliberately: `htp -w` (likewise `xdev -w`, `evade -w`, `ipp -w`).


Scanning
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
# Quick+thorough TCP, then targeted. (`nmapsweep <target>` is the helper's own take on
# #1 — `nmap -sCV -T4 -oA nmap/<target>`: unprivileged, so TCP-connect not SYN, and it
# writes into ./nmap/ rather than the <hostname>_N-* naming below. Not a wrapper.)
sudo nmap -sC -sV -oA <hostname>_1-initial <ip_address>
sudo nmap -sC -sV -sU -oA <hostname>_2-udp <ip_address>
sudo nmap -p- -T4 --min-rate 10000 -oA <hostname>_3-tcp-discovery <ip_address>
sudo nmap -A -p <ports> -oA <hostname>_4-tcp-full-scan <ip_address>   # <ports> from the discovery scan

# Faster port discovery when -p- drags:
rustscan -a <ip_address> --ulimit 5000 -- -sC -sV
masscan -p1-65535 <ip_address> --rate 10000 -oL masscan.txt

# Run -p- in a 2nd terminal so it never blocks the scripted scan; stagger to avoid collisions:
sleep 300 && sudo nmap -p- --min-rate 10000 -oA <hostname>_3-tcp-discovery <ip_address>
# UDP leaks creds — SNMP(161)/DNS(53)/TFTP(69). Stuck? unsticking playbook → ~/ippsec.

echo "<ip_address> <hostname> <domain>" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts   # so vhosts/Kerberos resolve
